"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"weird_trousers"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"I am done with articles stating &quot;I used this LLM to do that&quot;, or &quot;Look, this agent did that in 2 minutes!&quot;.\nI want content more user-centric, less openai / anthropic, and more &quot;human-in-the-loop&quot; articles.<p>Any other website to recommend?\nI think lobste.rs was a good hacker news aggregator, but I don't know if it is still relevant today...<p>UPDATE:<p>Some recommendations, provided by the answers:<p>- https://hackaday.com/  \n- https://hcker.news/?<em>ai</em>=exclude  \n- https://lobste.rs/<p>Related discussions<p>- https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48713041  \n- https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48567155"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"Ask HN: Another \"Hacker News\" with less <em>AI</em> and more human-focused hacking news?"}},"_tags":["story","author_weird_trousers","story_48834961","ask_hn"],"author":"weird_trousers","children":[48835569,48835755,48835348,48835183,48835301,48835425,48836742,48835795,48835882,48840443,48835947,48835297,48835390,48835500,48835836,48845998,48835725,48836655,48835858,48835856,48840108,48835426,48835217],"created_at":"2026-07-08T17:48:41Z","created_at_i":1783532921,"num_comments":54,"objectID":"48834961","points":92,"story_id":48834961,"story_text":"I am done with articles stating &quot;I used this LLM to do that&quot;, or &quot;Look, this agent did that in 2 minutes!&quot;.\nI want content more user-centric, less openai &#x2F; anthropic, and more &quot;human-in-the-loop&quot; articles.<p>Any other website to recommend?\nI think lobste.rs was a good hacker news aggregator, but I don&#x27;t know if it is still relevant today...<p>UPDATE:<p>Some recommendations, provided by the answers:<p>- https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hackaday.com&#x2F;  \n- https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hcker.news&#x2F;?ai=exclude  \n- https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lobste.rs&#x2F;<p>Related discussions<p>- https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=48713041  \n- https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=48567155","title":"Ask HN: Another \"Hacker News\" with less AI and more human-focused hacking news?","updated_at":"2026-07-18T23:34:17Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"santiagobasulto"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"Lately I've noticed that &quot;Who wants to be hired&quot; posts are getting more comments than the &quot;Who's hiring&quot; ones.<p>I pulled the data using HN API and confirmed this &quot;reversal&quot;. &quot;Who wants to be hired&quot; posts are getting 2x more comments than &quot;Who's hiring&quot; posts (and seems to be accelerating). For reference, in 2022 it used to be 0.25.<p>I'm old, I'm from the times where demand for software devs greatly outpaced supply, but maybe we're seeing a reversal of it?<p>I don't want to draw any conclusions yet, there could be many things going on. My initial thoughts would be <em>AI</em>-related. We're either entering a general recession because the <em>AI</em> bubble is bursting, or the job market is changing due to <em>AI</em>.<p>Again, I don't have any answers, but it's something definitively interesting to discuss!<p>Here's the source code (including data): https://github.com/santiagobasulto/hn-who-is-hiring-analysis"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Tell HN: Who wants to be hired\" posts outpace \"Who's hiring\" 2 to 1"}},"_tags":["story","author_santiagobasulto","story_48816813","ask_hn"],"author":"santiagobasulto","children":[48819657,48818689,48818567,48838333,48818407,48835268,48818417,48817732,48826578,48818646,48878665,48818891,48821137,48820439,48819176,48823274,48818283,48829738,48826781],"created_at":"2026-07-07T12:31:53Z","created_at_i":1783427513,"num_comments":35,"objectID":"48816813","points":57,"story_id":48816813,"story_text":"Lately I&#x27;ve noticed that &quot;Who wants to be hired&quot; posts are getting more comments than the &quot;Who&#x27;s hiring&quot; ones.<p>I pulled the data using HN API and confirmed this &quot;reversal&quot;. &quot;Who wants to be hired&quot; posts are getting 2x more comments than &quot;Who&#x27;s hiring&quot; posts (and seems to be accelerating). For reference, in 2022 it used to be 0.25.<p>I&#x27;m old, I&#x27;m from the times where demand for software devs greatly outpaced supply, but maybe we&#x27;re seeing a reversal of it?<p>I don&#x27;t want to draw any conclusions yet, there could be many things going on. My initial thoughts would be AI-related. We&#x27;re either entering a general recession because the AI bubble is bursting, or the job market is changing due to AI.<p>Again, I don&#x27;t have any answers, but it&#x27;s something definitively interesting to discuss!<p>Here&#x27;s the source code (including data): https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;santiagobasulto&#x2F;hn-who-is-hiring-analysis","title":"Tell HN: Who wants to be hired\" posts outpace \"Who's hiring\" 2 to 1","updated_at":"2026-07-20T08:32:36Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"yehiaabdelm"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"I'm a bit annoyed by the feeling that we're kind of stuck when it comes to using LLMs for programming.<p>I use Claude Code and Codex, but I haven't been able to enter flow state like I can when I hand write code.<p>This is kind of ironic to me since <em>AI</em> should be a bicycle for the mind, but right now it feels like a bicycle that just brakes abruptly every couple minutes. I stop, wait, review, prompt again.<p>Is there anyone exploring something fundamentally different than the prompt response loop we have today?<p>I actually think the idea of a tab model is directionally better than prompt response.<p>Would love to hear about any startups, personal experiments, etc."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Ask HN: Is anyone experimenting with different ways of using LLMs for coding?"}},"_tags":["story","author_yehiaabdelm","story_48771515","ask_hn"],"author":"yehiaabdelm","children":[48779784,48780140,48779017,48782072,48771907,48779404,48780818,48771922,48774103,48772037,48772023,48779308,48772274,48780664,48771844,48771558,48771908,48780254,48772049,48771846,48772031,48773615,48780323,48781568,48772067,48779870,48774635,48813788,48782539,48779198,48772655,48772050,48779159,48779429,48780572,48771813,48865107,48801622,48779519,48780725,48776921,48780615,48772171,48780665,48771940,48779774,48780635,48780512,48781102,48774147,48773463,48861468,48773801,48779629,48772253,48779307,48780148,48780663,48781607,48772551,48814494,48779953,48772025,48779985,48781823,48780687,48771836,48771910,48779833,48771969,48773778,48840418,48779215,48780877,48781699,48772203,48771683,48804058,48781629,48790124,48772113,48771785,48772201,48786954,48772226,48782641,48771738,48790263,48779375,48785505,48772149,48780190,48780621,48779767,48779892,48772247,48786289,48771982,48773409,48772574,48831094,48806894,48782296,48780544,48785971,48794004,48782408,48772013,48771644,48772527,48800273,48772632,48788537,48792568,48791893,48771945,48812754,48793079,48799908,48780848,48787253,48787133,48781470,48862733,48784499,48780079,48780259,48772301],"created_at":"2026-07-03T06:21:44Z","created_at_i":1783059704,"num_comments":206,"objectID":"48771515","points":212,"story_id":48771515,"story_text":"I&#x27;m a bit annoyed by the feeling that we&#x27;re kind of stuck when it comes to using LLMs for programming.<p>I use Claude Code and Codex, but I haven&#x27;t been able to enter flow state like I can when I hand write code.<p>This is kind of ironic to me since AI should be a bicycle for the mind, but right now it feels like a bicycle that just brakes abruptly every couple minutes. I stop, wait, review, prompt again.<p>Is there anyone exploring something fundamentally different than the prompt response loop we have today?<p>I actually think the idea of a tab model is directionally better than prompt response.<p>Would love to hear about any startups, personal experiments, etc.","title":"Ask HN: Is anyone experimenting with different ways of using LLMs for coding?","updated_at":"2026-07-17T09:35:12Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"sollawen"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"Here are my biggest gripes with <em>AI</em> coding assistants right now:\nObsessed with reinventing the wheel. You'll often find it writing three duplicate functions for the exact same feature in a single file. Why? Because it's terrified of blowing up the context window, so it only reads a fraction of a large file and completely misses the existing functionality.\nWhy are files so bloated in the first place? Because <em>AI</em> prefers adding new code over modifying existing code, and it rarely deletes anything. After a few iterations, your codebase becomes a mountain of dead code.\nZero holistic awareness. It hyper-focuses on the current task and couldn't care less if its changes break other parts of the system. If you point out that the original logic is now broken, it treats it as a brand-new task. It\u2019ll fix that specific bug, but whether its newly written code still works after the fix? &quot;Not my problem.&quot;\nThe context window is painfully short. If you try to solve the issues above by dumping all the necessary context into the prompt, you'll hit the 200k token limit in no time. After just two more turns, it triggers auto-compacting, and suddenly it turns back into an idiot.\nLong context = instant brain damage. Just when you've painstakingly stuffed the perfect amount of context into the window, the model completely loses its mind. The longer the context, the more incoherent its responses become.\nLogical reasoning is practically non-existent. A simple feature that would take a human dev 3-5 lines of code to tweak in an existing function? The <em>AI</em> will insist on designing an entirely new system architecture for it.\nThe more you tune it, the dumber it gets. You burn through millions of tokens, and every time it replies with &quot;I understand!&quot; or &quot;I've got it completely!&quot;, the code it spits out still refuses to run."},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"<em>AI</em> coding is a nightmare. Am I the only one experiencing this?"}},"_tags":["story","author_sollawen","story_48770319","ask_hn"],"author":"sollawen","children":[48772794,48770958,48773125,48773528,48772685,48773804,48771999,48772239,48778768,48773914,48862742,48772314,48772221,48772575,48772403,48781296,48794080,48771956,48774363,48774238,48772103,48774938,48770429,48772706,48771049,48773788,48774644,48772435,48772310,48772479,48770775,48777038,48772857,48770534,48771518,48773535,48776934,48772241],"created_at":"2026-07-03T03:18:42Z","created_at_i":1783048722,"num_comments":53,"objectID":"48770319","points":64,"story_id":48770319,"story_text":"Here are my biggest gripes with AI coding assistants right now:\nObsessed with reinventing the wheel. You&#x27;ll often find it writing three duplicate functions for the exact same feature in a single file. Why? Because it&#x27;s terrified of blowing up the context window, so it only reads a fraction of a large file and completely misses the existing functionality.\nWhy are files so bloated in the first place? Because AI prefers adding new code over modifying existing code, and it rarely deletes anything. After a few iterations, your codebase becomes a mountain of dead code.\nZero holistic awareness. It hyper-focuses on the current task and couldn&#x27;t care less if its changes break other parts of the system. If you point out that the original logic is now broken, it treats it as a brand-new task. It\u2019ll fix that specific bug, but whether its newly written code still works after the fix? &quot;Not my problem.&quot;\nThe context window is painfully short. If you try to solve the issues above by dumping all the necessary context into the prompt, you&#x27;ll hit the 200k token limit in no time. After just two more turns, it triggers auto-compacting, and suddenly it turns back into an idiot.\nLong context = instant brain damage. Just when you&#x27;ve painstakingly stuffed the perfect amount of context into the window, the model completely loses its mind. The longer the context, the more incoherent its responses become.\nLogical reasoning is practically non-existent. A simple feature that would take a human dev 3-5 lines of code to tweak in an existing function? The AI will insist on designing an entirely new system architecture for it.\nThe more you tune it, the dumber it gets. You burn through millions of tokens, and every time it replies with &quot;I understand!&quot; or &quot;I&#x27;ve got it completely!&quot;, the code it spits out still refuses to run.","title":"AI coding is a nightmare. Am I the only one experiencing this?","updated_at":"2026-07-20T05:58:36Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"seattle_spring"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"My LinkedIn feed is absolutely unreal right now. 90% (I don't even think I'm exaggerating) of the posts in my feed are from connections who have changed their title to something like &quot;<em>AI</em> Thought Leader | <em>AI</em> Native | Thought Coaching&quot;. They post *daily* about something LLM/agentic. The biggest problem is that it's never &quot;hey check out this cool thing&quot;, it's &quot;if you're not doing this, you're a dinosaur who will be left in the past.&quot;<p>Other common content is regurgitating blog / Twitter folks from &quot;<em>AI</em> influencers&quot;, associated with statements like, &quot;Boris McAI said software engineering is dead. Here's 5 reasons why he's right.&quot;<p>I felt this way a bit when cryptocurrency was at its height, but it never got this embarrassing or self-fellating. How do so many of my connections (more than 800 at this point) have literally zero shame? Hell it's not even first-degree connections."},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai"],"value":"Ask HN: Why are so many \"<em>AI</em> evangelists\" posting such insufferable content?"}},"_tags":["story","author_seattle_spring","story_48765450","ask_hn"],"author":"seattle_spring","children":[48767066,48766204,48768496,48767251,48787167,48795385,48767572,48796253,48776381,48806807,48766734,48768398,48800293,48768716,48765817,48765523,48766248,48772768,48798904,48767062,48769618,48772949,48773292,48769040,48767903,48773683,48790831,48774334,48771277,48772251,48856085,48782404,48766016,48768731,48775984,48766851,48765613],"created_at":"2026-07-02T18:23:03Z","created_at_i":1783016583,"num_comments":40,"objectID":"48765450","points":70,"story_id":48765450,"story_text":"My LinkedIn feed is absolutely unreal right now. 90% (I don&#x27;t even think I&#x27;m exaggerating) of the posts in my feed are from connections who have changed their title to something like &quot;AI Thought Leader | AI Native | Thought Coaching&quot;. They post *daily* about something LLM&#x2F;agentic. The biggest problem is that it&#x27;s never &quot;hey check out this cool thing&quot;, it&#x27;s &quot;if you&#x27;re not doing this, you&#x27;re a dinosaur who will be left in the past.&quot;<p>Other common content is regurgitating blog &#x2F; Twitter folks from &quot;AI influencers&quot;, associated with statements like, &quot;Boris McAI said software engineering is dead. Here&#x27;s 5 reasons why he&#x27;s right.&quot;<p>I felt this way a bit when cryptocurrency was at its height, but it never got this embarrassing or self-fellating. How do so many of my connections (more than 8